key-tap.oga: the click under each key of the on-screen keyboard (sounds.js).

Made by tools/make-key-tap.py, which synthesizes it from sines and noise: no recording, no sample from another
project. It is VibeOS's own, under the repository's Expat license (and CC0 for anyone who wants it on its own).
18 ms, mono, 48 kHz, Ogg Vorbis (4 KB). Run `tools/make-key-tap.py` to make it again; the noise is seeded, so the
samples are the same each time (the encoder's own bytes may differ with its version).

Measuring a key's latency on a device (sounds.js logs it)
---------------------------------------------------------

The shell logs each key tap and volume sound with CLOCK_MONOTONIC microseconds, when asked:

  touch $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vibeos-sound-log      # on (rm to stop); no restart: the shell watches for the file
  journalctl --user -f -o cat | grep vibeos-sound

(or VIBEOS_SOUND_LOG=1 in the shell's environment). A tap logs

  vibeos-sound: tap t=T play sent t=T2 (N us)
  vibeos-sound: tap t=T done t=T3 (M us)

T is the key going down (the entry of sounds.js keyTap(), called from the keyboard's press handler before the haptic
tick), T2 is the play having been handed to libcanberra (N is what it cost the shell's main loop), T3 is the click
having finished (about 18 ms of sound, so T3 - 18 ms is when its stream was done starting, or later). "silent (...)"
says why a tap or step was not played, "throttled" a volume step inside the 100 ms.

To see when the server starts the stream, run on the device at the same time, in a second shell (the python reads the
same clock as the log's t=):

  pw-mon | python3 -u -c 'import sys,time
  for l in sys.stdin:
      if l.startswith("added:") or "Key tap" in l:
          print(time.clock_gettime_ns(time.CLOCK_MONOTONIC) // 1000, l.rstrip())'

The line with the "Key tap" stream's properties minus T is the time from the tap to the server having the stream.
What follows is the audio graph's own delay: the sink's quantum (pw-top, column QUANT, over the rate) and the sound
card's buffer, which the logs cannot see. For the whole path, film the screen (the key preview shows as the key goes
down) with the speaker's click in the same clip, at 240 fps, and count the frames from one to the other.
